<article class="guide-content"><h1>The Total Compensation Equation: Cash, Benefits and Paid Leave</h1>
<p class="byline">Econified · Updated September 5, 2026 · <a href="/how-it-works/">Methodology and corrections</a></p>

<p>A higher salary can come with weaker benefits, but adding every benefit's headline value to salary creates another misleading number. Start with three comparisons: annual cash, employer benefits and time worked.</p>
<h2>Calculate annual cash</h2><p>Record base salary, a realistic bonus scenario and cash allowances. Keep guaranteed and variable amounts separate. Put a signing bonus in the first year only and record repayment conditions. For equity, use an annual vesting amount and test a zero-value scenario when its sale value is uncertain.</p>
<p>Illustrative Offer A has a $100,000 salary and $10,000 target bonus. Offer B has $110,000 salary and no bonus. Both reach $110,000 if A pays its full target; B provides $10,000 more salary if A's bonus pays nothing.</p>
<h2>Add benefits consistently</h2><p>Suppose A provides $4,000 in employer retirement contributions and a health benefit you value at $6,000. B provides $3,300 and $4,800 respectively. The gross package estimates become $120,000 and $118,100. A leads by $1,900 only under the stated bonus and benefit assumptions.</p>
<p>Do not add your own retirement contributions as an employer benefit. Check the contribution needed to receive a match and when you own employer contributions. The <a href="https://www.irs.gov/retirement-plans/plan-participant-employee/retirement-topics-vesting">IRS explanation of vesting</a> describes why the plan's ownership rules matter. For healthcare, compare premiums, coverage and expected out-of-pocket costs on the same basis.</p>
<h2>Compare paid leave without adding salary twice</h2><p>Paid leave is already paid through an annual salary. Do not add PTO days multiplied by daily salary as extra cash. Compare pay per hour actually worked and the leave policy itself.</p>
<p>For a simple five-day schedule, Offer A at $92,000 with 10 leave days gives $92,000 / (250 × 8) = $46.00 per worked hour. Offer B at $88,000 with 25 leave days gives $88,000 / (235 × 8) = $46.81. This example excludes separate holidays. B pays more per worked hour while A still pays $4,000 more annually.</p>
<h2>Apply the comparison</h2><p>Run the <a href="/job-offer-comparison/">job offer calculator</a> for the gross package and the <a href="/pto-value-calculator/">paid leave calculator</a> for working-time differences. Compare after-tax cash and commuting expenses separately. Personal priorities can change the choice even when one package has a higher estimated total.</p>

